<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
<title>Can a shin muscle signal predict the next pedal reading? · NeuroTranslate 0.0</title>
<style>@@STYLE@@
.inputs{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(200px,1fr));gap:14px;margin:10px 0}.inputs label{display:block}.inputs input{display:block;width:100%}figure{margin:.8em 0 1.2em;max-width:850px}canvas{width:100%;display:block;height:138px;background:#f0f3ec;border-radius:4px}figcaption{padding:.5em 0;color:#395347}.num{font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}textarea{min-height:3em}.controls{margin-top:.7em}
</style><main>
<nav aria-label="Related studies"><a href="https://eliminatenoise.github.io/NeuroTranslation/">NeuroTranslation</a><a href="https://eliminatenoise.github.io/NeuroTranslation/pedal-load/">Try the task-trained Transformer</a><a href="https://eliminatenoise.github.io/Interoceptomics/pedal-load/">Explore the apparatus</a></nav>
<header><h1>What will the pedal read next?</h1><p class="lead">A volunteer tries to lift their forefoot against a fixed pedal. Electrodes on the shin pick up a voltage from the muscle. A second sensor records the load on the pedal. Can the earlier muscle signal help predict a change in that reading?</p>
<p>These models see 201 consecutive samples of past measurements and forecast the load-channel change 100 sample positions later. The recording system sampled at a reported 1,000 times per second. Some files carry a time vector; for others, the nominal time is calculated from row order. Individual hardware intervals have not been verified.</p></header>
<section><h2>Change the invented signals</h2><p>Keep the blue pedal history fixed and vary the orange muscle signal. Only methods that use muscle voltage can respond to that change. The example values run entirely in your browser.</p>
<div class="inputs"><label>Recorded pedal setting <select id="setting"><option value="angle0">angle0</option><option value="angle5">angle5</option></select></label><label>Recent pedal trend <select id="trend"><option value="rising">Rising</option><option value="falling">Falling</option><option value="steady">Steady</option></select></label>
<label>Muscle signal size <input type="range" id="strength" min="0" max="2" step="0.1" value="1"><output id="strength-out"></output></label><label>Muscle burst location <select id="burst"><option value="early">Earlier in history</option><option value="late">Later in history</option></select></label></div>
<figure><canvas id="lines" width="840" height="170" aria-label="Past pedal load and rectified muscle signal on separate scales"></canvas><figcaption id="description"></figcaption></figure>
<details id="custom"><summary>Enter your own 201 sample pairs</summary><p>Enter one pedal reading and its matching muscle voltage on each line, separated by a comma, earliest first. Data stay in this browser. Values from the same source clock and the two supported channels are required for an interpretation.</p>
<label for="pairs">Load, muscle voltage</label><textarea rows="5" spellcheck="false" id="pairs"></textarea>
<div class="controls"><button type="button" id="apply">Calculate entered readings</button><button type="button" class="secondary" id="restore">Restore invented example</button></div></details>
<output id="status" aria-live="polite"></output><div class="table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">Forecast change</th><th scope="col">Later reading</th></tr></thead><tbody id="values"></tbody></table></div>
<p>The output uses the original load sensor’s scale. Converting it to force would require the measured calibration of the signal conditioner. The study’s 0.1 m platform lever could then convert calibrated force into a net ankle moment. It does not tell us the force generated by one muscle.</p>
<details><summary>How the response model works</summary><p>Take the absolute size of the muscle voltage at each sample. One running average responds quickly, another slowly. The response model learns a target load level from these two quantities and the pedal setting. It predicts part of the change toward that level over the next 100 samples. The chosen time constants describe a model assumption. They are not measurements of a volunteer’s muscle.</p></details></section>
<section><h2>What did the usable trials show?</h2><p id="scope"></p><p>Errors are mean absolute differences on the load channel’s original scale. Smaller is closer. Each person, setting and trial has equal weight at its own level. The two held participants supplied separate Task 1 recordings; their participant codes also occur in an earlier muscle-imaging study.</p>
<label>Choose a supported comparison <select id="group"><option value="complete:balanced">Complete recordings · both settings</option><option value="complete:angle0">Complete recordings · angle0</option><option value="complete:angle5">Complete recordings · angle5</option><option value="other_angle5:balanced">Second person · angle5 pair</option><option value="other_angle0:balanced">Second person · single angle0 trial</option></select></label><p id="selection"></p>
<div class="table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">Development error</th><th scope="col">New Task 1 recordings</th></tr></thead><tbody id="scores"></tbody></table></div><p id="finding"></p>
<p>For the wrong-trial check, the load history stays in place. The model gets the muscle signal from the other repetition by the same person at the same pedal setting. Choosing the corresponding part of the other repetition uses the completed trials. The test asks whether matching this particular muscle signal matters.</p></section>
<nav aria-label="Research and sources"><a href="https://eliminatenoise.github.io/NeuroTranslation/papers/pedal-load/">Read the study</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.6019/S-BIAD1482">Original recordings</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-024-04285-x">Original article</a></nav>
<script type="application/json" id="data">@@DATA@@</script><script>@@ENCODER@@
const data=JSON.parse(document.getElementById('data').textContent),model=data.model,$=id=>document.getElementById(id),fraction=-Math.expm1(-100/150);
function filtered(rect,tau){const a=-Math.expm1(-1/tau),z=[rect[0]];for(let i=1;i<rect.length;i++)z.push(z[i-1]+a*(rect[i]-z[i-1]));return z;}
function prep(l,e){const rect=e.map(x=>Math.abs(x)),fast=filtered(rect,25),slow=filtered(rect,80),t=[];
 for(let i=0;i<200;i+=50){const j=i+50;t.push([l[j],l[j]-l[i],l.slice(i,j+1).reduce((s,v)=>s+v,0)/51-(l[j]+l[i])/2,fast[j],slow[j],rect.slice(i,j+1).reduce((s,v)=>s+v,0)/51]);}
 return {t,d:[fast[200],slow[200],fast[200]-fast[100],slow[200]-slow[100]]};}
function estimate(m,l,e,setting){
 const current=l[200],recent=current-l[100],c=setting==='angle5'?1:0;
 if(m==='hold')return 0;if(m==='recent')return recent;if(m==='setting_median')return model.medians[setting];
 const f=model.fits[m],{t,d}=prep(l,e);let arr;
 if(m.startsWith('relaxation'))arr=m==='relaxation_setting'?[c]:[c,...d];
 else{const withEmg=['linear_both','shared_both','random_both'].includes(m);
  const z=t.map(row=>row.map((x,k)=>!withEmg&&k>=3?0:(x-f.token_mean[k])/f.token_sd[k]));
  arr=[...z.flat(),recent,m==='shared_load_no_setting'?0:c];
  if(!m.startsWith('linear')){const s=m==='random_both'?data.random:data.shared;arr.push(...encode(s,z.map(row=>linear(s,'project',row))));}
 }
 const adj=arr.reduce((s,v,i)=>s+(v-f.mean[i])/f.sd[i]*f.coef[i],f.intercept);
 return m.startsWith('relaxation')?fraction*(adj-current):recent+adj;
}
function row(body,label,values){const tr=document.createElement('tr');const th=document.createElement('th');th.scope='row';th.textContent=label;tr.append(th);for(const v of values){const td=document.createElement('td');td.textContent=v;tr.append(td);}body.append(tr);}
function trace(l,e){const can=$('lines'),ctx=can.getContext('2d');ctx.clearRect(0,0,840,170);
 const lines=[[l,'#1c6279',65],[e.map(x=>Math.abs(x)),'#a34515',148]];
 for(const [v,color,bottom] of lines){const lo=Math.min(...v),hi=Math.max(...v),span=hi-lo||1;ctx.strokeStyle=color;ctx.lineWidth=2;ctx.beginPath();v.forEach((v,i)=>{const x=12+i*4.05,y=bottom-48*(v-lo)/span;if(i===0)ctx.moveTo(x,y);else ctx.lineTo(x,y);});ctx.stroke();ctx.font='12px sans-serif';ctx.fillStyle=color;ctx.fillText(color==='#1c6279'?'Pedal load':'Rectified muscle signal',12,bottom+16);}
 $('description').textContent='Separate vertical scales. The load runs from '+Math.min(...l).toFixed(3)+' to '+Math.max(...l).toFixed(3)+' in this history. The graphs end at the forecast cutoff.';
}
function clear(){ $('values').replaceChildren();$('lines').getContext('2d').clearRect(0,0,840,170);$('description').textContent='';}
function show(l,e,origin){clear();const predictions=Object.keys(data.labels).filter(m=>m!=='wrong_both').map(m=>[m,estimate(m,l,e,$('setting').value)]);
 if(predictions.some(([,v])=>!Number.isFinite(v)||!Number.isFinite(v+l[200]))){$('status').textContent='These values exceed the model range.';return;}
 trace(l,e);for(const [m,v] of predictions)row($('values'),data.labels[m],[v.toFixed(4),(l[200]+v).toFixed(4)]);
 $('status').textContent=origin==='custom'?'Forecasts from the 201 entered pairs.':'Forecasts from the invented example.';}
function invented(){const strength=Number($('strength').value),trend=$('trend').value,b=$('burst').value==='early'?62:154;
 const l=[],e=[];for(let i=0;i<=200;i++){let v=.38+(trend==='steady'?0:(trend==='rising'?1:-1)*.0019*i);v+=.012*Math.sin(i*.058);l.push(v);e.push(strength*(.055+.75*Math.exp(-((i-b)**2)/620))*Math.sin(i*.51));}
 $('strength-out').textContent=strength.toFixed(1)+' ×';$('pairs').value=l.map((v,i)=>v.toFixed(8)+', '+e[i].toFixed(8)).join('\n');show(l,e,'invented');}
function custom(){clear();try{const pairs=$('pairs').value.trim().split(/\r?\n/).map(s=>s.split(',').map(v=>v.trim()?Number(v):NaN));if(pairs.length!==201||pairs.some(x=>x.length!==2||!x.every(Number.isFinite)))throw Error('Enter exactly 201 rows with two finite numbers on every line.');show(pairs.map(x=>x[0]),pairs.map(x=>x[1]),'custom');}catch(e){$('status').textContent=e.message;}}
function score(){
 const [id,mode]=$('group').value.split(':'),g=data.test.groups[id],selected=g.scores[mode],devMode=id==='other_angle5'?'angle5':id==='other_angle0'?'angle0':mode;
 $('scores').replaceChildren();
 for(const [m,label] of Object.entries(data.labels))row($('scores'),label,[data.dev.scores[devMode][m].mae.toFixed(4),selected[m]?selected[m].mae.toFixed(4):'Unavailable']);
 const count=mode==='balanced'?g.windows:g.windows_by_setting[mode];
 $('selection').textContent=count+' eligible forecasts. '+(g.has_wrong_trial?'Another trial at the same setting supplies the comparison signal.':'This recording has no qualified partner for the wrong-trial comparison.');
}
let editing=false;
for(const id of ['trend','strength','burst'])$(id).addEventListener('input',()=>{if(editing){$('strength-out').textContent=Number($('strength').value).toFixed(1)+' ×';$('status').textContent='Your entered readings are kept. These controls change only the invented example: choose Restore invented example to go back to it.';return;}invented();});
$('setting').addEventListener('input',()=>{if(editing)custom();else invented();});
$('pairs').addEventListener('input',()=>{editing=true;clear();$('status').textContent='Values changed. Calculate the entered readings.';});
$('apply').addEventListener('click',()=>{editing=true;custom();});$('restore').addEventListener('click',()=>{editing=false;$('strength').value=1;$('trend').value='rising';$('burst').value='early';invented();});$('group').addEventListener('change',score);
$('scope').textContent=data.scope;$('finding').textContent=data.finding;invented();score();
</script></main></html>
